---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/unification-of-italy-and-germany">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson builds on two foundational History lessons. How historians work gave you
    the habit of weighing interpretations against evidence — a habit you will use in the
    section asking how far Bismarck was responsible. The medieval world showed how both
    Italy and Germany were left as patchworks of separate states, which is precisely the
    map the statesmen of the 1850s and 1860s set out to overcome.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why the revolutions of 1848–49 failed in both Italy and Germany, and what each failure taught the next generation;</li>
    <li>trace Cavour's diplomatic road from Plombières to the plebiscites, and weigh whether he helped or hindered the movement;</li>
    <li>describe Garibaldi's Thousand, the conquest of the south, and the gift at Teano;</li>
    <li>follow Bismarck's three short wars from Denmark to the Hall of Mirrors, and say what each one was for;</li>
    <li>argue, with evidence, how far Bismarck was responsible — and how far structures made unification happen anyway.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1848, Italians and Germans tried to build nations out of barricades — and lost. Within
    twenty-three years both countries existed as united states anyway, made by statesmen,
    soldiers and plebiscites instead of revolutionaries. That is the puzzle: why did
    1848–49 fail, and what did the next generation do differently? The short answer is that
    Italy and Germany were unified <em>from above</em>, by existing states that captured the
    nationalist movement rather than being captured by it. Piedmont-Sardinia became the engine
    of Italy; Prussia became the engine of Germany. The results were shaped by very different
    methods — Italy by diplomacy, volunteers and plebiscites, twice needing foreign armies;
    Germany by three short, calculated wars launched by one man with an army purpose-built for
    the job.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each engine with its work" widget="match" config={uniEnginesMatch} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="two unifications, one timeline — filter by nation with the chips" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the events one at a time, then use the
    chips to filter by nation and watch the story split into two strands. Notice how the
    strands interlock anyway: Italy gains Venetia in 1866 only because Prussia defeats
    Austria, and Rome falls in 1870 only because France is busy fighting Prussia. Keep one
    question in mind as you read on — who is acting at each step, a people or a state?
  </p>

  <h2>Italy: why 1848–49 failed</h2>
  <p>
    After the Congress of Vienna (1815) "Italy" was a set of separate states: Piedmont-Sardinia
    in the north-west, Austrian-ruled Lombardy-Venetia across the north, three central duchies
    under Habsburg-related rulers, the Papal States, and the Bourbon Kingdom of the Two
    Sicilies in the south. Metternich called it "a geographical expression". In 1848–49
    Italians tried to dissolve that expression by revolution — and failed everywhere.
    Piedmont's King Charles Albert declared war on Austria and was beaten at Custoza (1848)
    and Novara (1849); Venice's republic starved into surrender in August 1849; Mazzini's
    Roman Republic, with Garibaldi defending it, was crushed by French troops sent to restore
    the Pope. Four reasons recur: Austrian military power (Radetzky's veterans beat every
    coalition thrown at them); division (republicans and monarchists had no common plan, and
    the Pope refused to lead a war on Catholic Austria); shallow popular support (the
    revolutions were urban and middle-class; most peasants stayed home); and conservative
    great powers — even France intervened <em>against</em> an Italian republic. Yet one
    outcome mattered enormously: Piedmont alone kept its constitution, the Statuto, because
    Victor Emmanuel II refused to revoke it. It became the one liberal, constitutional Italian
    state and the refuge for exiles — hence the lesson of the 1850s: a state, not a street,
    would make Italy.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each cause of the failure" widget="sort-buckets" config={uni1848Buckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> 1848 showed that popular revolutions could not beat Austrian
    armies or unite divided Italians — but Piedmont's surviving constitution handed the next
    generation a legal, liberal state to build with. The man who would use it was Cavour.
  </p>

  <h2>Cavour: the statesman's road — help or hinder?</h2>
  <p>
    Count Camillo di Cavour, Prime Minister of Piedmont from 1852, supplied the method: make
    Piedmont so modern and respectable that Europe would back it as Italy's natural leader.
    He industrialised the state — railways, banks, free trade — then bought a seat at the top
    table, sending an army corps of some 15,000 men to the Crimean War (1855) and presenting
    "the Italian question" as a European question at the Congress of Paris (1856). The
    masterstroke was the secret pact of Plombières (July 1858): France would help expel
    Austria from northern Italy; Piedmont would pay with Savoy and Nice. In 1859 Austria
    blundered into an ultimatum, and the allies won at Magenta (4 June) and Solferino
    (24 June) — whose 40,000 casualties so horrified a Swiss witness, Henri Dunant, that he
    founded the Red Cross. Then Napoleon III got cold feet: alarmed by Prussian mobilisation
    and Catholic outrage over the Pope, he signed the Villafranca peace alone. Piedmont gained
    Lombardy; Venetia stayed Austrian. Cavour resigned in fury, returned in January 1860, and
    consolidated: plebiscites in Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna joined Piedmont in
    March 1860, France taking Nice and Savoy as its price.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="line up Cavour's road, earliest first" widget="order" config={uniCavourOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Cavour planned Italian unification from the start. He did
    not. His stated aim in 1859 was a Kingdom of Upper Italy — Piedmont plus Lombardy-Venetia
    plus the duchies, with the Pope keeping Rome; the whole peninsula joining was improvised
    by events, above all by Garibaldi. Did Cavour help or hinder? He helped by giving the
    movement an army, a legal framework of plebiscites and international respectability; he
    hindered by ceding Nice (which enraged the Nizzard Garibaldi), by first trying to stop
    Garibaldi's expedition before adopting it, and by channelling everything into a
    conservative monarchy with no place for Mazzini's republicans. He died in June 1861,
    three months after the Kingdom was proclaimed — Venetia and Rome still missing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag the divider — aim, then outcome" widget="compare" config={uniAimCompare} />

  <h2>Garibaldi: the Thousand and the gift</h2>
  <p>
    Giuseppe Garibaldi supplied the drama. In May 1860 he sailed from Quarto with the
    "Thousand" — 1,089 red-shirted volunteers, many students and artisans — and landed at
    Marsala in western Sicily. What followed was wildly improbable: victory at Calatafimi
    (15 May) against a larger force, the fall of
    Palermo at the end of May despite a garrison of some 20,000 troops, a crossing to the
    mainland in August, and the occupation of Naples on 7 September. The Bourbon army of
    some 100,000 men dissolved through desertion and defeat. Then came the decision that
    shaped a century: rather than march on Rome as a revolutionary, Garibaldi met Victor
    Emmanuel II at Teano (26 October 1860) and handed over the south with the words "I obey",
    refusing every honour. The Kingdom proclaimed the following March spanned the peninsula
    because of that gift.  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the number — the Thousand" widget="estimate" config={uniThousandEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Garibaldi unified Italy. He conquered the south — about a
    third of the new state — but the north was won by Cavour's diplomacy and the regular
    Piedmontese army, and the centre by plebiscites held after Piedmontese troops marched in.
    Even the expedition could only sail because Cavour's annexations had destabilised the
    map, and Victor Emmanuel's army crossed the Papal States (beating papal forces at
    Castelfidardo, 18 September 1860) to meet him. Garibaldi's later attempts on Rome failed
    — wounded by Italian troops at Aspromonte (1862), checked at Mentana (1867). Rome fell in
    the end to diplomacy: with France fighting Prussia in 1870, its garrison went home, and
    Bersaglieri breached the walls at Porta Pia on 20 September 1870. Venetia had
    arrived in 1866, as Prussia's ally, despite Italian defeats at Custoza and Lissa.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="find the faulty line — who made Italy" widget="spot-error" config={uniGaribSpot} />

  <h2>Germany: why 1848–50 failed</h2>
  <p>
    Germany's revolution took a parliamentary form. The Frankfurt National Assembly — some 600
    delegates, almost all lawyers, professors and officials —
    met from May 1848 to draw up a united Germany. It produced an admirable Bill of Fundamental
    Rights, but it had no army, no treasury, no executive: every order had to be requested
    from the princes, who simply waited it out. Worse, it wasted months on the leadership
    question that would haunt German liberalism for seventy years: a <em>kleindeutsch</em>
    Germany led by Prussia with Austria excluded, or a <em>großdeutsch</em> one including
    Austria? In April 1849 it offered the imperial crown to Frederick William IV, who refused
    a crown "offered by a parliament" — the crown from the gutter. The Humiliation of Olmütz
    (November 1850) then forced Prussia to abandon its own union plan and restore the old
    Austrian-chaired Confederation. Revolution by committee had failed for the same reasons
    as in Italy: no force, no unity, no mass base.
  </p>
  <p>
    But the 1850s quietly built the preconditions. The Zollverein customs union, founded in
    1834 under Prussian leadership, had by 1866 enrolled every German state except Austria:
    economic unity already existed, with Austria deliberately outside. The railway network
    more than tripled — from about 5,900 km in 1850 to about 19,000 km in 1870 — knitting the
    states together and giving Prussia's general staff a new weapon: armies moved by rail
    faster than enemies could march. Middle-class nationalism reorganised legally through the
    Nationalverein (1859). And Prussia reformed its army from 1859 under Roon and Moltke:
    universal service, a professional general staff, and the Dreyse needle gun, a
    breech-loading rifle that fired several times faster than the Austrian muzzle-loader.
    When Bismarck arrived in September 1862 — appointed during the crisis over army spending,
    and taxing without parliamentary approval for four years — the machine was waiting for a
    driver.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="the railway argument, 1850–70" widget="plotter" config={uniRailPlotter} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> Germany's failure in 1848–50 was parliamentary where Italy's
    was military, but the 1850s rebuilt both countries from above — Italy through Piedmont's
    respectability and plebiscites, Germany through the Zollverein, railways and a reformed
    army. Now the driver arrives to test the machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Bismarck's method: Denmark and Austria, 1864–66</h2>
  <p>
    Bismarck's first war was fought <em>with</em> Austria. When Denmark tried to detach
    Schleswig from Confederation rules in 1863, Prussia and Austria jointly invaded (1864),
    and the Gastein Convention (1865) split administration of the duchies — the awkward seam
    between them left deliberately to fester. Meanwhile Bismarck prepared the real target:
    Russian goodwill (Prussia had helped crush the 1863 Polish revolt), an Italian alliance
    (April 1866, dangled with the promise of Venetia), and French neutrality bought at
    Biarritz (October 1865) with vague promises of "compensation" never defined. When the
    Bund voted to mobilise against Prussia on 14 June 1866, Bismarck declared it dissolved
    and marched. Moltke's railways and needle guns decided the Seven Weeks' War: three
    Prussian armies converged on Bohemia and crushed the Austrians at Königgrätz (3 July
    1866) — roughly 250,000 Prussians against some 200,000 Austrians and Saxons; Prussian
    losses about 9,000, Austrian about 44,000 including 20,000 captured. Then came the move
    that marks Bismarck as a statesman: a deliberately <em>lenient</em> peace. The Peace of
    Prague (August 1866) cost Austria no German territory and almost no indemnity — it only
    excluded Austria from German affairs for good. Prussia annexed the north-German states
    that had opposed it (Hanover, Nassau, Hesse-Kassel, Frankfurt) and in 1867 founded the
    North German Confederation, a federal state with a parliament elected by universal male
    suffrage — democratic window-dressing on Prussian control. The leniency was strategy:
    Austria stood aside in the next war, as it did in 1870.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="isolate Austria — make the purchases" widget="branch" config={uniBismarckBranch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Bismarck had a master plan worked out from 1862. He
    improvised at every step — the Danish war was opportunistic, the Gastein split a stopgap,
    and in 1866 his own king wavered so badly about fighting Austria that he considered
    abdicating. Historians such as A.J.P. Taylor argue that Bismarck rode events more than he
    steered them — but with a consistent direction of travel: every crisis ended with Prussia
    stronger and Austria weaker.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bismarck's real constraint was arithmetic, not diplomacy: Prussia could not be strong
    everywhere at once. Drag the arrow to feel the problem:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="interior lines: point Prussia’s army at one front and the other goes thin" widget="diagramBuilder" config={frontsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the arrow starts pointing mostly east — the historical
    1866 posture. Drag the tip and watch the trade-off appear: every unit of force you add
    to one front is force the other front loses, and the magnitude readout totals what
    Prussia actually has to spend.
  </p>

  <p>
    Read the axes as <strong>x = force massed west against France, y = force massed
    south-east against Austria</strong>. The starting arrow, pointing mostly east, is the 1866
    posture: the main mass against Austria, a thin screen on the Rhine — safe only because
    Bismarck had bought French goodwill. Drag it mostly west for 1870: the main mass against
    France, the east exposed — safe only because Austria had been beaten <em>and leniently
    treated</em> in 1866. Now try making both components 10: the magnitude readout shows 14 —
    no Prussia possessed that army. Unification was the art of never pointing the arrow both
    ways at once.
  </p>

  <h2>The war with France, 1870–71</h2>
  <p>
    The southern states had stayed out of the Confederation, tied to Prussia by trade and
    held back by fear of France. Bismarck needed a war that would make them run to him — and
    Napoleon III provided it. In 1869–70 the Spanish throne was offered to Leopold, a prince
    of the Catholic Hohenzollern line; France, horrified at Hohenzollerns on two frontiers,
    protested until Wilhelm I withdrew the candidacy. But the French ambassador was then sent
    to demand, at Bad Ems, a guarantee that it would never be revived. Wilhelm refused
    politely and wired an account home; Bismarck abridged the telegram, published it on
    13 July 1870, and made it read as an insult to France — "a red rag to the Gallic bull".
    A jingoistic Parisian press pushed Napoleon III into declaring war on
    19 July. No ally came: Austria hesitated, Russia remembered Poland, Italy resented Rome,
    Britain stayed out.
  </p>
  <p>
    The French army was outmanned and out-organised; Prussia's mobilisation by rail was
    flawless, France's a shambles. After defeats at Wörth, Mars-la-Tour and Gravelotte, the
    Army of Châlons was trapped at Sedan: on 1–2 September 1870 Napoleon III surrendered with
    some 83,000 men. A republic was proclaimed in Paris on 4 September, but the war went on —
    Metz capitulated on 27 October with around 170,000 men, and Paris endured a four-month
    siege before surrendering in January 1871. On 18 January 1871,
    in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, the German princes proclaimed Wilhelm I German
    Emperor: a deliberate wound, inflicted in the palace of France's own kings. The Treaty of
    Frankfurt (10 May 1871) confirmed it — France ceded Alsace and most of Lorraine
    (including Metz and Strasbourg) and owed 5 billion francs, with German troops occupying
    the country until payment. The southern states had already joined: the war they feared
    had delivered them, gift-wrapped, to Prussia.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the telegram alone caused the war. Bismarck edited the Ems
    dispatch, but he did not invent the crisis — the candidacy was real, and French alarm at
    it was strategically rational. What the telegram did was convert a withdrawal into a
    public humiliation at the exact moment French opinion demanded glory. Wars need spark and
    fuel; Bismarck supplied only the spark.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two kingdoms now exist, both made by states rather than streets
    — Italy completed in 1870, Germany proclaimed in 1871. What remains is judgement: how
    much of this was Bismarck, and how much would have happened anyway?
  </p>

  <h2>How far was Bismarck responsible?</h2>  <p>
    Weigh Bismarck against the structures. The Zollverein, the railways, the army reforms
    (Roon and Moltke began before he took office), the organised nationalism of the
    Nationalverein, and the diplomatic conjuncture — Russia weakened by Crimea (1856) and
    distracted by Poland (1863), Austria humiliated in Italy (1859), France complicit in its
    own isolation — all pointed towards a Prussian-led Germany. Without Bismarck, unification
    might have happened anyway; without him it might equally have stalled for a generation.
    What he decided were the things at the margin: the timing, the <em>kleindeutsch</em> form
    (Austria excluded for good), the leniency to Austria, the baiting of France, and —
    catastrophically — the annexation of Alsace-Lorraine against the advice of Moltke and the
    Crown Prince. The fairest verdict: unification was overdetermined, but Bismarck chose its
    speed, shape and price.
  </p>

  <h2>What unification meant for Europe</h2>
  <p>
    Italian unification mattered most to Austria (expelled from Italy, though it kept the
    Italian-speaking Trento and Trieste that nationalists called <em>Italia irredenta</em>)
    and to France, which gained Nice and Savoy but spent a decade defending the Pope — a
    growing source of Franco-Italian friction until 1870 removed the garrison. Britain was
    broadly sympathetic. German unification
    was the earthquake. Austria, expelled from Germany, reorganised as the Dual Monarchy with
    Hungary (1867) and turned south-east. Denmark and France had lost territory; France gained
    a permanent grievance — <em>revanche</em> — that drove its diplomacy for forty years.
    Above all, a new empire of some 41 million people now fielded the best army in Europe.
    Bismarck spent the next two decades building alliances precisely because unification had
    frightened everyone; Alsace-Lorraine remained the fault line along which Europe cracked
    in 1914.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name two reasons the Frankfurt Parliament failed to unite Germany in 1848–49.</li>
    <li>Why did Bismarck impose a deliberately <em>lenient</em> peace on Austria in 1866?</li>
    <li>The timeline shows Italy gaining Venetia in 1866 despite losing at Custoza and Lissa. How did Venetia arrive?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The Assembly had no army, no treasury and no executive,
    so the princes simply waited it out; and it wasted months on the leadership question —
    kleindeutsch versus grossdeutsch — before offering Frederick William IV a crown he
    refused because it came from a parliament. (2) To keep Austria from fighting again: the
    Peace of Prague excluded Austria from German affairs but took almost no territory, and
    in 1870 Austria stood aside — the leniency was strategy. (3) As Prussia's ally: the
    April 1866 alliance dangled Venetia as the price, and Austria's defeat at Königgrätz
    removed the power that held it — France handed Venetia over despite the Italian defeats.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Both unifications were made from above: existing states captured the nationalist movement instead of being captured by it.</li>
    <li>The revolutions of 1848–49 failed for recurring reasons — great-power armies, divided opposition, shallow popular support.</li>
    <li>Cavour's method was to make Piedmont modern and respectable, buy great-power help, and legalise expansion through plebiscites.</li>
    <li>Garibaldi won the south; the gift at Teano made the Kingdom of Italy possible in March 1861.</li>
    <li>Each of Bismarck's wars served one purpose: Denmark secured the duchies, Austria expelled it from German affairs, France brought the south in.</li>
    <li>The two unifications were coupled, not parallel — each settlement set up the next.</li>
    <li>The price of 1871 — Alsace-Lorraine and revanche — was the fault line along which Europe cracked in 1914.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling intermediate lessons take the two ends of this story further: Revolutions of
    1848 examines the failed revolution-from-below this lesson presupposes, and The Treaty
    of Versailles shows what happened when the Germany created in the Hall of Mirrors lost
    the next war — with The League of Nations following the consequences of that peace.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Notice on the timeline that the two unifications were not parallel events but
    <em>coupled</em> ones: 1859, 1864, 1866 and 1870–71 form a single chain in which each
    settlement set up the next. Villafranca's unfinished business (Venetia) drew Italy into
    1866 on Prussia's side; the leniency of 1866 kept Austria neutral in 1870; Rome fell in
    September 1870 only because France's garrison was needed at Sedan. Even Italy's completion
    was a by-product of Germany's method — one reason historians treat 1848–71 as a single
    process, nationalism reshaping Central Europe through the great-power system.
  </p>
  <p>
    The new states carried costs that triumphal maps hide. The Kingdom of Italy began with a
    franchise of under 2 per cent (about 421,000 voters out of 22 million), a decade-long
    campaign against "brigandage" in the south, and a north-south wealth gap (the
    <em>questione meridionale</em>). The German Empire was a constitutional oddity: a Kaiser
    controlling the army, a Bundesrat where Prussia held 17 of 58 votes, and a Reichstag
    elected by universal male suffrage that could not dismiss governments. Historians call
    1871 a "revolution from above": the nation was made, but parliament was not sovereign —
    an imbalance that shaped German politics until 1918.
  </p>

